/* ==============================================================
   02 — TRF: the Tool Reference Frame
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   TEACHES   SetTrf — telling the robot where the tool tip is
   NEEDS     Robot activated. Runs safely with no tool.
   WATCH     A turn about the shaft, then about a tool tip that
             is off to one side of it
   TIME      ~30 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE OTHER HALF OF THE FRAME STORY

   The WRF answers "where is the work?". The TRF answers "where
   is the tool?".

       SetTrf(x, y, z, γ)

   defines the TRF — the Tool Reference Frame — with respect to
   the FRF, the Flange Reference Frame. On the MCS500 the flange
   is simply the lower end of the spline shaft: the FRF sits on
   the very end of it, z up along the shaft axis.

   By default the TRF sits exactly there. That is correct and
   useless: nothing does work at the end of a bare shaft. The
   work happens at the lip of the suction cup, the point of the
   dispensing needle, the centre of the gripper's jaws —
   somewhere below the shaft, and sometimes off to one side of
   it.

   Tell the robot where that point is, and two things become
   true:

   1. Every pose you write refers to the TOOL TIP, not the shaft.
      You program the point that touches the part.

   2. Turns happen ABOUT THE TIP. This is the one that matters
      and the one that demonstrates well. Ask the robot to turn
      the tool 40 degrees and it pivots around the tip of the
      needle, instead of swinging the needle through an arc as
      the shaft turns.

   THE TCP

   The origin of the TRF is the TCP — Tool Centre Point. When a
   customer says "our TCP", this command is what they mean. (Not
   to be confused with TCP/IP, which is unfortunate but
   universal.)

   THE SIGN THAT CATCHES EVERYONE

   Every frame on this robot has its z axis pointing UP, and the
   robot will not accept anything else. Tools hang DOWN from the
   shaft. So the z of almost every real TRF is NEGATIVE:

       SetTrf(0, 0, -30, 0)    tip 30 mm below the end of the shaft

   If you come from a six-axis robot, where the tool frame's z
   usually points out of the tool, this is backwards from what
   your fingers expect. Write it down once and check it every
   time.

   And γ? It turns the tool frame about the vertical — useful to
   make the tool's x axis run along a gripper's jaws or a
   camera's image rows. For a round tool like a suction cup it
   does nothing you would notice; leave it at 0.

   WHERE THE NUMBERS COME FROM

   Three sources, in increasing order of effort:
     - the tool's own drawing, if it was designed for this
     - measurement of the built assembly
     - a touch-off routine, jogging the tip to a known point at
       several turns of the shaft

   THE HOUSE TOOL

   Most SCARA tools sit on the shaft axis — a suction cup, a
   small gripper, a pusher — so the tip is straight below the
   flange, with no sideways offset. This repo uses one such tool
   throughout:

       SetTrf(0, 0, -30, 0)

       0, 0   the tip is on the shaft axis
       -30    and 30 mm below the end of the shaft

   The 30 is a stand-in. Measure from the end of the spline shaft
   to the working point of YOUR tool, and use that. This line is
   the house tool for every example from here on.

   WHY IT PAYS OFF LATER

   Change the tool, change one line, and every pose in every
   program is still correct. Without a TRF, a new tool means
   reteaching every point. This is the argument that lands with
   anyone who maintains a cell rather than builds one.

   Like the WRF, the TRF resets to its default when the robot is
   activated or reactivated. And like SetWrf, SetTrf brings the
   robot to a complete stop at that point in the queue, even with
   blending on.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode, MoveJump
   settings — so this program starts from firmware defaults no
   matter what ran before it. Reactivating brings the motors
   back.

   This does NOT move the arm: the MCS500 has absolute encoders
   and never needs homing, which is why there is no Home() here.

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision, P-Stop 2 and calibration config
   all survive. Run 00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(15)
SetCartLinVel(60)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- Before: no tool defined -----------------------------------
   TRF sits on the end of the shaft, so poses describe the end of
   the shaft. */
SetTrf(0, 0, 0, 0)

MovePose(170, 0, 60, 0)
Delay(1)

// Turn 40 degrees about the TRF's z axis, then back. With no
// tool defined, that axis is the shaft itself — so only joint 4
// moves, and the shaft spins on the spot. Anything mounted off
// to one side of it sweeps through an arc.
//
// MoveLin, not MovePose: a MoveLin keeps the TCP on a straight
// line — here, a line of zero length — so the pivot really does
// hold still for the whole move, not just at the two ends.
MoveLin(170, 0, 60, 40)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLin(170, 0, 60, -40)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLin(170, 0, 60, 0)

Delay(1)


/* --- After: an off-axis tool defined ---------------------------
   Imagine a dispensing needle on a small bracket: its tip 40 mm
   out to the side of the shaft axis, along the FRF's x, and
   30 mm below the end of the shaft. Off-axis tools like this —
   needles, probes, a camera beside a cup — are where the TRF
   stops being a formality. No physical tool is required to see
   the effect. */
SetTrf(40, 0, -30, 0)

// Move the TIP to the same place the end of the shaft was
// before. The arm visibly draws back 40 mm and the shaft rises
// 30 mm to put it there, which on its own tells the story: the
// robot is now positioning the tool, not itself.
MovePose(170, 0, 60, 0)
Delay(1)

// The same 40 degrees about the TRF's z axis — which now runs
// vertically through the needle tip. The tip stays put and the
// SHAFT orbits around it: joints 1 and 2 swing the arm while
// joint 4 turns, all three together. This is the moment the
// concept lands for most people; it is much more convincing
// seen than described.
MoveLin(170, 0, 60, 40)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLin(170, 0, 60, -40)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLin(170, 0, 60, 0)


// --- Put the tool back ----------------------------------------
SetTrf(0, 0, 0, 0)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Run the two halves back to back with no narration. "Watch
      what the tip does" is the only line you need.

   2. Change the 40 in SetTrf to 60 — a longer bracket — and run
      again. Longer tool, same code, still pivots about the tip.
      Then ask what changing a tool costs them today.

   3. If a real off-axis tool is mounted, measure it, put the
      real offsets into SetTrf, and touch the tip to a fixed
      point. Turn in place and show the tip staying on that
      point through the turn. This is the single most persuasive
      thing you can do with a SCARA on a table.

   NEXT   03_relative_moves.mxprog — moving relative to the tool
          and to the world, which is what the frames really buy
          you.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
